lesson writing-speech-bubbles v=1 format=teach grade=2 unit=4 subject=writing levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Speech bubbles"
  goal "Write what a character says in a bubble, with the mark that matches how it is said."

  look {
    scene 35x13 {
      comic strip who=["girl", "fox", "girl"] says=["Is that a fox?", "Yes, and I am hungry!", "Then have my apple."] moods=["surprised", "excited", "happy"] with=["", "", "fox"] thinks=[0, 0, 0] holds=["", "", "apple"] labels=["1", "2", "3"] at=canvas(1, 1)
    }
    say """
      In a comic the words people say go in a bubble, and the tail points at who is talking. A
      bubble still needs a capital letter and a mark at the end. The girl asks, so her first bubble
      ends with a question mark. The fox shouts, so its bubble ends with an exclamation mark.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked writing.bubble-mark k=1 level=medium
    }
    practice writing.bubble-mark count=1 seed=2
    practice writing.reply-fits count=2 seed=3
    practice writing.capitals-count count=2 seed=1
    practice writing.same-words-moods count=2 seed=4
    practice writing.speech-order count=1 hard-count=0 seed=2
    level hard {
      practice writing.same-words-moods count=1 seed=2
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show writing.fill-the-bubble k=0
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show writing.two-bubbles k=1
  }
  remember {
    say "Words that are said go in a bubble. The mark shows how they are said."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The marks, the replies, the capitals and the order are checked by the machine. When the same
    words sit in two bubbles, only the faces can say which one is shouted, so point at the faces if
    the child guesses. The bubbles in the two-star and three-star questions are the child's
    writing: what matters is that they join the picture before to the picture after, which is the
    first step towards writing speech in a story. Read them aloud in the voices of the people.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level a shout's mark is chosen once in the teacher's pen before a question's or a telling's, the replies answer where, who and what, the words that need a capital are the first word and one name, the moods and the order are as written, and the two-star and three-star are as written."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the mark for bubble 2 ends a shout or a question, the replies answer when and what, the words that need a capital are two names, I and a name in the middle, the order makes way for a second pair of moods, and the two-star and three-star are as written."
  }
}
